## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are comprised of metal ions/clusters and organic ligands using coordinate bonding to form peculiar structures, which have emerged as a new class of materials due to its multi functionalities, high surface area, and permanent porosity [1,2]. The hybrid of inorganic and organic parts in the MOFs makes them suitable candidates for various applications such as gas sensing and separation, gas storage, catalysis, energy production, electronics, optics, and water purification [3,4]. MOFs have been explored for environmental remediation and monitoring applications. However, some shortcomings such as low light absorption capacity, poor electrical conductivity, inefficient charge transfer, rapid electron-hole recombination, and recyclability limit their application [5–8]. The disadvantages of MOF can be lessened by changing the types of ligands, mixed metal centers and/or ligands, or combining the MOF with other functional materials to form nanocomposites [9]. The consolidation of the MOF and functional materials not only improves the functional properties but also expands the area of applications. The synergetic effect in the composite may provide new features which are not achievable by individual components [10–13]. Various MOF composites have been prepared with different functional materials including polymers [14], platinum nanoparticles [15], metal oxides [16], biomolecules [17], carbon nanotubes [18], graphene oxide [19], and perovskite quantum dots [20].
Plasmonic materials are also added in the MOFs to form novel composites due to the existence of localized surface plasmon resonance and improved charge transfer [15]. Jalali et al. synthesized a plasmonic MOF photocatalyst by coupling $\mathsf{A g_{2}C r O_{4}\mathrm{-AgCl/Ag}}$ plasmonic nanoparticles and HKUST-1 MOF via a hydrothermal method [21]. ${\sf A g_{2}C r O_{4}{\mathrm{-}}\sf A g C l/A g}$ heterostructure was used as a Zscheme system given that the single form of metallic Ag was not suitable to use alone due to its photosensitivity and slight solubility in the solvents. Likewise, Miao et al. developed the $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}125$ (Ti)/Ag plasmonic composite to form the Schottky junction and induce the surface plasmon resonance effect at the interface for the enhanced photodegradation of ketoprofen [22]. However, these synthetic approaches for plasmonic MOF composites usually consist of at least three components and experience some common disadvantages such as sophisticated synthesis processes, poor photostability, and high fabrication cost [15,21–24].
Degenerately doped semiconductors are emerging plasmonic candidates because their plasmonic properties can be tailored by varying dopant concentrations or sub-stoichiometry [25–27]. Upon the incorporation of a large number of dopants into the crystal structure, the concurrently injected free charge carriers enter into the electronic band structure of the host, forming gap states and eventually shifting the Fermi level position above that of the conduction band minimum (CBM) or below that of the valence band maximum (VBM) [27,28]. Consequently, this so-called ‘‘degenerate doping” results in the transition of the host from semiconducting to quasi-metallic. The small variation of the charge carrier concentration in degenerate semiconductors causes a significant effect on the spectral position of localized surface plasmon resonance, which is more appealing compared to those of conventional plasmonic noble metals [29]. $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ ions as the smallest ions in nature can be incorporated as dopants, which can modify the electronic band structure of the host and induce the insulator-to-metal transition [30]. Molybdenum oxide is an intrinsic $n$ -type semiconductor and can be doped with a large amount of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ ions to form into the plasmonic type III $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ ( $1.55<\mathbf{x}<1.72;$ with surface plasmon resonance peaks in the visible light region, which is highly desirable for the photocatalysis applications as visible light is around $43\%$ of the entire solar spectrum [26,28,31,32]. The concurrently injected electrons in $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ during the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ addition process not only results in the generation of visible surface plasmon resonance [28] but the highly negative surface as well, which can be beneficial to physisorption-based gas sensing dominated by the interfacial charge transfer [33]. These features alongside the advantages of a relatively environmentally benign, simple synthesis, and low fabrication cost create a plasmonic composite of ultrathin oxides with MOF having a strong potential for real-world implementation.
In this paper, FeBDC $\mathrm{\BDC}=1$ , 4-benzenedicarboxylic acid) MOF (FeMOF) is used to form the composite, due to readily available cost-effective precursors, non-toxic iron metal, and eminent physiochemical features [34,35]. FeMOF is a breathing and flexible MOF with the capability of reversible contraction and relaxation of the framework’s unit cell without damaging the topology. The changes in conformation are driven by external stimulus and evidenced by the material’s different surface areas between the dried and wet conditions [36,37]. The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}–\upmu_{3}–0\uptimes0$ cluster is linked with the BDC linker to form a hybrid structure [38]. Herein, we synthesize the core–shell $\mathrm{H_{x}M o O_{3}/F e M O F}$ plasmonic composite (FeMOFC) using a facile hydrothermal method. Such a novel plasmonic MOF composite is characterized and evaluated for liquid-phase environmental remediation and gas-phase environmental monitoring. The liquid-phase environmental remediation performance is evaluated through the investigation of visible-light-driven photobleaching properties against various dyes (azo and non-azo). For the gasphase environmental monitoring, the electrical conductivity of the composite is investigated upon the exposure of paramagnetic $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas as the target analyte.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
All chemicals (Iron (III) nitrate nonahydrate $(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $299.95\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich), terephthalic acid $(\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{H}_{4^{-1}},4\mathsf{-}(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathsf{H})_{2}$ , $98\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich), tartaric acid $\mathrm{^{.}C_{4}H_{6}O_{6}}$ $299.5\%$ , SigmaAldrich), and ammonium molybdate tetrahydrate $((\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{6}\mathrm{Mo_{7}O_{24}-}$ $4\mathrm{H}_{2}0,\geq99.9\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich) were purchased from the commercial seller and used without further purification.
## 2.2. Preparation of ultra-thin $H_{x}M o O_{3}$
The ultra-thin $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ was prepared by dissolving the $250~\mathrm{mg}$ of ammonium molybdate tetrahydrate $(0.2~\mathrm{mM})$ and $120~\mathrm{mg}$ of tartaric acid $(2~\mathrm{mM})$ in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of distilled water at room temperature under magnetic stirring. The solution with a pH value of 2.5 was transferred to a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and placed in an oven at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After the synthesis, the sample was washed thoroughly with water and dried under the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ environment [28].
## 2.3. Preparation of FeMOF and FeMOFC
FeMOF was prepared by the hydrothermal synthesis method. ${\sim}1\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(2.5~\mathrm{mM})$ was mixed in a $10~\mathrm{mL}$ aqueous dispersion consisting of ${\sim}0.9\ \mathrm{\g}$ of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ $\cdot5.4\mathrm{\mM})$ and $_{0.36\mathrm{~g~}}$ of ultra-thin $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ under magnetic stirring for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . The Fe and Mo atom ratio was 1:1 in the solution. After mixing, the solution was transferred to a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and maintained in an oven at $200~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The product was centrifuged at 4000 rpm for $15\ \mathrm{min}$ , subsequently washed carefully with distilled water several times, and finally activated in a vacuum oven at $80^{\circ}C$ [39]. Similarly, FeMOF (FeBDC) was also prepared in a similar process except for the introduction of ultrathin $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ .
## 2.4. Characterization techniques
The morphological structure of samples was studied by highresolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM, JEOL 2100F) at $200\ \mathrm{kV}$ and scanning electron microscopy (SEM, FEI Quanta 200) at $30~\mathrm{kV}$ . Oxford $\mathbf{x}$ -MaxN 20 energy dispersive $\mathsf{X}.$ -ray spectrometer (EDS) at SEM was used to perform elemental mapping of the prepared samples. X-ray diffraction (XRD) measurements were carried out in Bruker D4 Endeavour containing a CuKa X-ray source with a wavelength of $1.5\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ at the operation current and voltage of $40\ \mathrm{\kV}$ and $40\ \mathrm{\mA}$ , respectively. Fouriertransform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) spectra were conducted on Perklin Elmer Spectrum 100 FT-IR spectrometer ranging between 600 and $4000\ c m^{-1}$ . Raman spectroscopy was performed using the Horiba Scientific LabRAM HR evolution excited using a 50 mW $532~\mathrm{nm}$ laser. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) data were collected using a Thermo Scientific K-Alpha XPS spectrometer equipped with a monochromatic Al $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\pmb{\upalpha}}$ X-ray source $\mathrm{\Delta}^{\cdot}\mathrm{hv}=1486.7$ eV). The UV–Vis absorption spectra were collected using an Agilent Cary 60 Spectrophotometer. The Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) measurements were carried out using an Asylum MFP-3D Infinity Atomic Force Microscope coped with a Multi75E-G tip. All images were processed using Gwyddion $\mathtt{v}2.47$ to measure the contact potential difference $(\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{CPD}})$ for determining the work function.
## 2.5. Photobleaching experiments
The visible-light-driven photobleaching performance of FeMOF and FeMOFC were conducted for different azo (congo red, basic blue 41, trypan blue, methyl orange) and non-azo (methylene blue, rhodamine 6G) dyes at a $10\upmu\mathrm{M}$ concentration. For the experiment, $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of dye solution was added to a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ glass vial. Then $2~\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst was added to the solution and stirred to evaluate the adsorption (30 min in dark) and photobleaching activity (180 min under visible light irradiation) of both FeMOF and FeMOFC for each dye. The output light of the solar simulator was 1 Sun. GLORIA-X150A (150 W Xenon light) and a UV cutoff filter $\langle\lambda>420~\mathrm{nm}\rangle$ were employed. The free radicals scavenger experiments were performed using $1\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ sodium azide, benzoquinone and tert-butanol for capturing the singlet oxygen, superoxide, and hydroxyl radicals, respectively.
## 2.6. Gas sensing studies
$1\%$ wt carbon black was added to ethanol and sonicated for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ to form solution A. On the other hand, FeMOF or FeMOFC was added to ethanol and sonicated for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ to form solution B. The solution A and B were mixed and underwent further sonicated for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ to make a homogenous mixture. $0.6~\mathrm{mg}$ of the mixture was dropped cast on the inter-digital electrodes (IDEs) for gas sensing investigation. The gas sensing tests were conducted in a
LINKAM stage (Scientific Instruments, UK) with a multichannel flow controller (MKS 1479A, U.S.A) for regulating the gases at a constant flow rate. The tests were performed by the exposure of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas at room temperature and the selectivity investigation was performed compared to various gases (e.g. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ , and ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}S{\mathrm{;}}$ .
## 3. Results and discussions
## 3.1. Morphological and structural characterization
The prepared pure FeMOF and composite are in powder form with reddish-brown and yellowish-brown color, respectively. In order to confirm the presence of the FeMOF and FeMOFC structures, extensive characterizations have been performed. The morphological structure is investigated by SEM and TEM (Fig. 1 a, c, d, f), demonstrating the average size of FeMOF and FeMOFC is ${\sim}3.0$ and ${\sim}16.7~\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ , respectively based on statistical analysis (Fig. S1 a, b). The typical core–shell structure is observed, in which $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ (circled in black) is embedded into the FeMOF structure according to Fig. 1f. Moreover, HRTEM images from the insets in Fig. $\textsf{1c}_{}$ , f show the highly-crystalline nature of FeMOF. Such a feature is maintained even after the formation of the core–shell composite. EDS mapping in Fig. 1 b confirms the presence of Fe, C, and O elements in FeMOF, while Mo element is distributed homogeneously across FeMOFC (Fig. 1 e). These results indicate the successful incorporation of $\mathsf{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{M o O}_{3}$ in the MOF structure while maintaining the high crystalline feature of the FeMOF host.
The Raman spectra of FeMOF and FeMOFC samples are mostly consistent with the FTIR spectra as shown in Figs. 2c and S2. The spectra of both samples between 600 and $1800~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are predominant by the vibration modes of the organic ligand except the broader peaks at around 735 and $962~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the composite [47,48]. The stretching vibration of $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ and $c{\mathrm{-}}c$ in the benzene ring is observed at 1630 and $1450~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. The $c{-}0$ bond vibration is observed at 1124 and $1182~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , while the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bending bands are found at 805 and $835~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in both samples [42,48]. The bending vibration of the OCO (carboxylate group) is observed at $631~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [49]. The peaks between 250 and $450\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ represent the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}$ cluster in the frameworks [49,50]. The absence of the peak around $1044~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ indicates that no nitrate ion from the precursor salt is left in the samples [49]. The peak at $520~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ appears due to the Si substrate. The broader peaks at
962 and $737~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are typical signature for $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ (in Fig. 2c), which can also be found in FeMOFC [27,51,52].
XPS is utilized to evaluate the chemical compositions of FeMOF, $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ , and FeMOFC. The XPS survey spectra of FeMOF and FeMOFC in Fig. $S3\texttt{a}$ , b show that the prepared samples have no other elemental peak except C, O, Fe, and C, O, Fe, Mo, respectively, confirming no observable impurity in samples. The C1s peak at $284.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ was used as a reference for the peak shift correction. In Fig. 3a, C 1s spectra of FeMOF and FeMOFC indicate the $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}{\mathsf{/}}$ ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-H}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{=}}{\mathsf{C}}$ and $\scriptstyle0=\mathsf{C}-0$ bonds in the structure [43,53]. The O 1s spectra of FeMOF, $\mathsf{H}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{M o O}_{3}$ and FeMOFC are shown in Fig. 3b. The peak at ${\sim}532.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ indicates the presence of O component in the BDC linker. The peak centered at ${\sim}530.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ is attributed to the
OAMo and OAFe in the FeMOFC and OAMo in the $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ [44,52], respectively. The peak centered at ${\sim}530.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ in FeMOF spectra indicates the presence of the O-Fe bond in the structure [54]. In Fig. 3c, the doublet peaks appear at ${\sim}711.3$ (Fe $\scriptstyle2{\mathrm{p}}_{3/2}.$ ) and \~724.8 eV (Fe $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}\mathrm{.}$ ) for FeMOF and ${\sim}711.6$ (Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}.$ ) and ${\sim}725.2\mathrm{eV}$ (Fe $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}\mathsf{.}$ ) for FeMOFC, which are attributed to Fe-O bond because of Fe species of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0$ cluster [55,56]. The shift of both peaks in FeMOFC compared to pure FeMOF is attributed to the variation in the local environment in the presence of Mo [57]. Fig. 3d depicts the Mo 3d peaks at ${\sim}232.6$ $(3\mathsf{d}_{5/2})$ and ${\sim}235.5\ \mathrm{eV}\ (3\mathrm{d}_{3/2})$ for both $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ and FeMOFC samples, which are attributed to the MoAO bond [52,58]. From Fig. S4, the deconvolution analysis of the Mo 3d peaks indicate the existence of both ${\mathrm{M}}{\mathrm{o}}^{4+}$ and ${\mathrm{M}}{\mathrm{o}}^{5+}$ oxidation states in both samples due to the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ doping and concurrently injected electrons in the molybdenum oxide host of $\mathsf{H}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{M o O}_{3}$ [59]. Interestingly, the ${\mathrm{M}}{\mathrm{o}}^{5+}$ oxidation state becomes more dominant over that of ${\mathrm{M}}{\mathrm{o}}^{4+}$ in the composite.
The XPS valence band spectra in Fig. 4a indicates the valence band maximum (VBM) of FeMOF and FeMOFC are 0.93 and $1.99\mathrm{eV}$ away from the Fermi level, respectively. Given by the optical bandgap energy of $4.05\ \mathrm{~eV}$ estimated from the Tauc plot (Fig. S5a), FeMOF exhibits a $p$ -type semiconducting feature. It is also noted that there is a hump between 1 and $1.5\mathrm{eV}$ in FeMOFC, ascribed to the existence of Mo 4d state in $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ . Such observation also suggests the quasi-metallic nature of the composite [28].
Further, UV–Vis spectroscopy is employed to investigate the optical absorption properties of materials. The intrinsic absorption peaks due to the MOF structure are below $300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in both FeMOF and FeMOFC as shown in Fig. S6. However, when in the visible light region according to Fig. 4b, two additional absorption peaks are found in FeMOFC, indicating the presence of surface plasmon resonance originated from $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ [28].
## 3.2. Visible-light-driven photobleaching performance
The extended absorption range in FeMOFC enables visible-lightdriven photocatalysis [38]. Its photocatalytic performance is examined by the visible-light-driven photobleaching of various dyes including mono-azo, di-azo and non-azo, in which FeMOF is used as the benchmark for comparison without the conventional incorporation of oxidative agents $\left(\mathrm{e}.\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}0_{2}\right)$ [19,60]. Methyl orange and basic blue 41, congo red and trypan blue, as well as methylene blue and rhodamine 6G, are chosen as the representatives of mono-azo, di-azo, and non-azo dyes, respectively. The change in dye concentration is shown against ${\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}_{\mathrm{{o}}}$ , where C is the concentration of dye at any time and $C_{\phantom{}_{0}}$ is the initial concentration at $\mathbf{t}=0$ . The photobleaching performances of both FeMOFC and FeMOF are shown in Fig. 5a-f. The photobleaching performances of FeMOFC are $81.73\%$ , $37.1\%$ , $20.9\%$ , $84.2\%$ and $35.1\%$ for congo red, basic blue 41, trypan blue, methylene blue and rhodamine 6G, respectively, which is significantly improved compared to those of FeMOF $55.4\%$ , $29.9\%$ , $8.5\%$ , $61.6\%$ and $27.9\%$ for congo red, basic blue 41, trypan blue, methylene blue and rhodamine 6G, respectively). Both FeMOF and FeMOFC show negligible photocatalytic activity against methyl orange. The optical absorption of FeMOF is mostly up to $350~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which is not efficient to harvest the visible light. Therefore, it is reasonably assumed that the photobleaching activity of FeMOF under visible light irradiation is mostly due to the surface absorption, given by the ‘‘breathing” nature of FeMOF [4,34,36]. On the other hand, the improvement in the photobleaching performance of FeMOFC is ascribed to the plasmonic absorption of $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}-$ $\mathsf{M o o}_{3}$ in the visible region [28]. The free radicals capture experiments were conducted using sodium azide, benzo-quinone, and tert-butanol as the scavengers as shown in Fig. S7. It is found that the introduction of tert-butanol during the methylene blue photobleaching significantly degrades the performance, suggesting that the hydroxyl radical (_OH) plays a dominant role over other free radicals for the dye photobleaching. We further obtain the electronic band structure of FeMOFC in Fig. S9, in which the Fermi level position, bandgap energy, and the distance between VBM and the Fermi level are determined by KPFM (Fig. S8), Tauc plot (Fig. S5b), and XPS valence spectrum (Fig. 4a), respectively. From Fig. S9, the VBM position is very close to the energy required for _OH production [60], matching with the conclusion drawn from the free radicals capture experiments.
## 3.3. Gas sensing performance
The gas sensing performance of FeMOFC is also evaluated at room temperature, while FeMOF is used as the control. Oxidizing $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ diluted in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ gas, which is known as a hole donor [61], is selected as a target analyte. The response factor of a sensor is the ratio of change $(\Delta{\sf R}={\sf R}_{\sf g a s}–{\sf R}_{0})$ in resistance to the resistance of baseline $\left(\mathsf{R}_{\circ}\right)$ [62].
$$
\mathrm{Response(R)}=(\mathrm{R_{gas}-R_{o})/R_{o}}
$$
where the ${\tt R}_{0}$ is the baseline resistance and $\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{gas}}$ is the resistance after the exposure of target gas.
From Fig. 6a, c, both FeMOF and FeMOFC are $\mathfrak{p}$ -type gas sensing materials as their electrical resistances decrease in the presence of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas. The dynamic responses of these samples indicate good reversibility for $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas ranged from 0.06 to 1 ppm. However,
FeMOFC exhibits almost three times larger for the response factor compared to that of FeMOF at 1 ppm $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ . In the case of FeMOF, the metal centers provide the interaction between the gas and the MOF due to electron deficiency [63]. Upon the exposure of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas, charge transfer occurs between FeMOF and the surface adsorbed gas molecules, which results in the increase of hole concentration in the MOF body and therefore improves the electrical conductivity. On the other hand, the desorption of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas molecules dominates over the recovery phase under the oxygen-minimum environment. When incorporated with electron-rich $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ , the physisorption of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas results in the formation of surface dipoles with the Drude-model-like electrons in $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ , leaving holes in the body. Therefore, hole conduction paths can be possibly formed between FeMOF and $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ , further increasing the electrical conductivity. Moreover, Fig. 6b, d show the selectivity of FeMOF and FeMOFC to 1 ppm $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ are shown in the presence of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , and $\mathsf{H}_{2}S$ , respectively. While poor selectivity is demonstrated for FeMOF possible due to the non-discriminative nature of the metal centers, FeMOFCs exhibit almost five times larger in terms of response magnitude on $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ compared to other gases. FeMOFCs exhibit almost $52\%$ , $396\%$ and $176\%$ larger in terms of response magnitude on $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ compared to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ , respectively.
## 4. Conclusions
We successfully synthesized a novel plasmonic MOF nanocomposite through the direct incorporation of ultra-thin degenerate $\mathsf{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{M o O}_{3}$ in the flexible FeMOF structure using a hydrothermal approach, which was relatively facile and cost-effective compared to those of noble metal-based plasmonic MOF nanocomposites with sophisticated structures [21–23]. The composite was with a core–shell structure, in which $\mathsf{H}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{M o O}_{3}$ acted as the plasmonic core encapsulated by FeMOF. While FeMOF demonstrated a $p$ -type semiconducting feature, the quasi-metallic nature of degenerate $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ enabled the surface plasmon resonance in the composite, generating additional absorption peaks in the visible light region. Through the demonstration of photobleaching performances of various azo and non-azo dyes under the irradiation of UV-filtered solar light, pure FeMOF was found to be visible-light inactive with limited dye photobleaching originated from the surface absorption. The plasmon-induced visible light-harvesting capability of FeMOFC resulted in enhanced photocatalytic performances without the incorporation of oxidative agents compared to those of pure FeMOF [19,60]. Furthermore, we extended the investigation of the unique functionalities of FeMOFC in the gaseous phase through the demonstration of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas sensing in the oxygen minimum environment at room temperature. The exposure of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ to both FeMOFC and FeMOF decreased the electrical resistance, confirming their $p$ -type semiconducting feature. FeMOFC exhibited almost three times larger in response magnitude toward 1 ppm $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ with the near-full reversibility compared to that of pure FeMOF. In addition, the response to $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ of FeMOFC was up to $50\%$ larger compared to those of industry commonly-seen gases including $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , and $\mathsf{H}_{2}S$ , while there was poor selectivity for pure FeMOF [64,65]. Such improved gas sensing performance was possibly ascribed to the electron-rich surface of $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ . The absorbed $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ formed surface dipoles by accepting the electrons from $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ . As a result, the charge carriers of $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ were significantly re-distributed and the body hole conductivity was therefore increased. The successful demonstration of plasmonic MOF nanocomposites based on hydrogen doped molybdenum oxides will provide a viable way to incorporate emerging plasmonic degenerate semiconductors into the highly porous MOF structure to include novel functionalities for high-performance energy harvesting, catalytic and sensing applications.
